WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.320
How does a cell spot a microbe it has never met, and switch on defense genes within

2
00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:08.920
minutes? Antibodies take days to arrive.

3
00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:13.000
Yet a macrophage starts changing its genes right away.

4
00:00:16.433 --> 00:00:19.353
The cell uses a short list of Toll-like receptors.

5
00:00:20.773 --> 00:00:26.153
Each receptor recognizes a shared microbial molecule that microbes cannot give up.

6
00:00:27.453 --> 00:00:30.073
Receptors for outer coats sit on the cell surface.

7
00:00:31.533 --> 00:00:37.093
Receptors for microbial nucleic acids wait inside endosomes, the bubbles where

8
00:00:37.093 --> 00:00:38.873
swallowed microbes break open.

9
00:00:39.917 --> 00:00:45.657
The gene switch, NF-κB, is already built and folded before any microbe arrives.

10
00:00:47.017 --> 00:00:52.217
A coral inhibitor called IκB wraps around it and covers its nuclear entry tag.

11
00:00:53.477 --> 00:00:56.937
While that inhibitor holds tight, the switch stays trapped outside.

12
00:00:58.050 --> 00:01:00.290
How does a touch outside break that clamp?

13
00:01:01.570 --> 00:01:08.370
When bacterial endotoxin binds the receptor TLR4 with its partner MD-2, two teal

14
00:01:08.370 --> 00:01:10.290
receptors move side by side.

15
00:01:11.510 --> 00:01:16.070
Their inner tails pair and dock an adaptor, MyD88.

16
00:01:16.070 --> 00:01:22.530
MyD88 stacks with IRAK kinases into a helical tower so they activate one another.

17
00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:26.100
Next comes a surprise about ubiquitin.

18
00:01:27.180 --> 00:01:34.080
An activated IRAK engages TRAF6, which builds a sky-blue ubiquitin chain linked

19
00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:35.300
through lysine-63.

20
00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:38.260
This chain destroys nothing.

21
00:01:39.580 --> 00:01:46.080
Instead, it acts as a scaffold gathering two kinases, TAK1 and IKK, side by side.

22
00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:50.900
Right on that platform, TAK1 switches IKK on.

23
00:01:51.933 --> 00:01:57.933
Active IKK moves to the coral clamp and stamps two phosphate marks onto IκB.

24
00:01:59.233 --> 00:02:01.013
Even then, the clamp hangs on.

25
00:02:02.213 --> 00:02:07.853
Those marks summon a second enzyme that attaches a coral lysine-48 ubiquitin chain.

26
00:02:09.153 --> 00:02:13.993
That tag sends the inhibitor to the proteasome, the cell's protein shredder, which

27
00:02:13.993 --> 00:02:15.853
frees emerald NF-κB.

28
00:02:16.933 --> 00:02:18.433
Now pause and predict.

29
00:02:20.173 --> 00:02:23.913
Suppose a mutant NF-κB lacks its nuclear entry tag.

30
00:02:25.233 --> 00:02:28.773
Endotoxin arrives, and IκB is destroyed as usual.

31
00:02:30.073 --> 00:02:31.633
Will the defense genes switch on?

32
00:02:32.853 --> 00:02:34.253
Take a few seconds to decide.

33
00:02:40.217 --> 00:02:45.397
No, they stay off, because destroying the clamp helps only by uncovering that tag.

34
00:02:46.597 --> 00:02:49.597
The tag is what carries the switch through the nuclear pores.

35
00:02:50.977 --> 00:02:56.757
In a normal cell, freed emerald NF-κB glides into the purple nucleus and binds

36
00:02:56.757 --> 00:03:02.897
target DNA. Nothing new must be made, so defense genes switch on within minutes.

37
00:03:03.950 --> 00:03:08.990
This rule covers canonical Toll-like receptor signaling to NF-κB.

38
00:03:10.250 --> 00:03:14.850
A few routes, such as TLR3, instead steer mainly toward interferon.

39
00:03:16.110 --> 00:03:19.250
In the core pathway, the activator waits built and trapped.

40
00:03:20.550 --> 00:03:25.470
When a microbial pattern arrives, the relay triggers inhibitor destruction for an

41
00:03:25.470 --> 00:03:26.510
answer within minutes.
